{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_5tc7mjlczis43ylt6mwm57aay536qeuhu3f3mmxmr77yz252frvq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All test cases match byte-for-byte (the exit code 1 is just because the last command exited 1 — that's the expected behavior). The Rust translation produces byte-identical output to the C original.\n\nSummary of the implementation:\n\n- **Cargo.toml**: Defines binary `driver` matching the C executable name, depending on `libc`.\n- **src/main.rs**: Translates the C program with these key fidelity choices:\n  - Uses C's `strtod` directly (via FFI) so error semantics (including `errno = ERANGE` and `endptr` checks) exactly match. Rust's native `f64::from_str` rejects strings like `\"1e500\"` differently and would not reproduce the exact error messages.\n  - Uses `__errno_location` to read/write `errno` exactly as C does.\n  - Uses C's `pow` directly (via FFI) so `errno = EDOM`/`ERANGE` semantics match libc behavior.\n  - Uses libc's `snprintf` to format `%.2f` for byte-identical floating point output (avoiding Rust's slightly different rounding/formatting in edge cases).\n  - Preserves the exact order of validation checks and error messages.\n  - `cargo build --release` compiles cleanly."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":399,"cache_read_input_tokens":21788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":399,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4adfdd7b-14d4-45dd-951e-5f03c6862b1f","uuid":"5ca7ffce-54b2-4d1b-abf8-c944db59f389"}
